// =============================================================================
// yolo_axi_arbiter.sv — 4-to-1 mux onto the single AXI4 master port
//
// The socket in Top.vhd gives the accelerator exactly ONE AXI master. Four
// things want it: the sequencer (descriptor fetch), the conv engine, the
// elementwise engine, and the detect engine.
//
// Fixed priority is correct here and simpler than round-robin, because the
// sequencer serializes the engines by construction -- it never starts a second
// engine while one is running, so at most one engine requester is ever active
// at a time. The arbiter therefore never actually arbitrates between engines;
// it only muxes. Priority exists so that a design error (two engines live at
// once) degrades into a deterministic stall rather than corrupted interleaved
// bursts on the bus.
//
// Grant is LOCKED for the whole transaction. Releasing on rd_done/wr_done
// alone would be wrong: a new requester could win the port between the address
// phase and the data phase and interleave its beats into another master's
// burst. The lock is held from rd_start/wr_start to the matching done.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REQUESTS ARE LATCHED, NOT MUXED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Requesters assert *_rd_start / *_wr_start for exactly ONE cycle. An earlier
// version muxed that pulse straight through, choosing the winner
// combinationally so it reached the master in the same cycle. That works only
// while the channel is unlocked. If a requester pulses start while the lock is
// held by someone else, the pulse goes nowhere: the master never sees the
// request, no done can ever come back, and that requester waits forever.
//
// The claim that this could not happen -- "the sequencer serializes the
// engines, so at most one requester is ever active" -- overlooked that the
// SEQUENCER IS ITSELF REQUESTER 0. It issues the next descriptor fetch one
// cycle after an engine reports done, while that engine's final burst can
// still hold the lock (rd_locked clears on m_rd_done, which lands later). The
// fetch pulse was dropped and the sequencer parked in S_FETCH_W with busy=1
// and error=0 -- indistinguishable from a slow layer, and dependent on exactly
// how the last burst lined up, which is why it moved between builds.
//
// So each requester's start is now LATCHED into a pending bit along with its
// address and length, and issued when the channel frees. A pulse can no longer
// be lost. m_*_start becomes a registered one-cycle pulse (one cycle later
// than before, which no requester can observe); write DATA and STRB stay
// combinational on the granted requester, because those belong to the data
// phase and must track it beat by beat.
//
// sim/tb_yolo_arbiter.sv (in qrts) is the regression: test 3 has an engine
// take the channel and the sequencer request mid-burst.
// =============================================================================
`timescale 1ns/1ps

module yolo_axi_arbiter (
    input  wire        clk,
    input  wire        rst_n,

    // ---- requester 0: layer sequencer (descriptor fetch) ----
    input  wire        s0_rd_start, input wire [63:0] s0_rd_addr, input wire [7:0] s0_rd_len,
    output wire [63:0] s0_rd_data,  output wire s0_rd_data_valid, output wire s0_rd_done, output wire s0_rd_error,
    input  wire        s0_wr_start, input wire [63:0] s0_wr_addr, input wire [7:0] s0_wr_len,
    input  wire [63:0] s0_wr_data,  input wire [7:0] s0_wr_strb,  output wire s0_wr_data_ready, output wire s0_wr_done, output wire s0_wr_error,

    // ---- requester 1: conv engine ----
    input  wire        s1_rd_start, input wire [63:0] s1_rd_addr, input wire [7:0] s1_rd_len,
    output wire [63:0] s1_rd_data,  output wire s1_rd_data_valid, output wire s1_rd_done, output wire s1_rd_error,
    input  wire        s1_wr_start, input wire [63:0] s1_wr_addr, input wire [7:0] s1_wr_len,
    input  wire [63:0] s1_wr_data,  input wire [7:0] s1_wr_strb,  output wire s1_wr_data_ready, output wire s1_wr_done, output wire s1_wr_error,

    // ---- requester 2: elementwise engine ----
    input  wire        s2_rd_start, input wire [63:0] s2_rd_addr, input wire [7:0] s2_rd_len,
    output wire [63:0] s2_rd_data,  output wire s2_rd_data_valid, output wire s2_rd_done, output wire s2_rd_error,
    input  wire        s2_wr_start, input wire [63:0] s2_wr_addr, input wire [7:0] s2_wr_len,
